  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Zeros of a Transfer Function",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute zeros of a rational transfer function by solving its numerator polynomial and interpreting the roots as s-plane locations where $H(s)$ vanishes.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Zeros of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Zeros of a Transfer Function",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A transfer function is written in rational form as $H(s)=\\frac{N(s)}{D(s)}$. In this notation, $N(s)$ is the numerator polynomial and $D(s)$ is the denominator polynomial, so the move is about reading the numerator structure in the right ratio form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{N(s)}{D(s)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To locate zeros, set the function value to zero. If $H(s)=0$, then the numerator must be zero: $H(s)=0 \\Rightarrow N(s)=0$, with the transfer function remaining defined.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=0 \\Rightarrow N(s)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This condition is valid only when the denominator is not zero, so $D(s)\\neq 0$ at the candidate point. Otherwise the expression is not a finite value of $H(s)$ and is not a zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)\\neq0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base example $H(s)=\\frac{s+3}{s+1}$. For this step, set the numerator equation to zero: $s+3=0$, then solve to get $s=-3$. Since $-3$ does not make $s+1$ equal zero, it is an allowed zero value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Each root of $N(s)$ that also satisfies $D(s)\\neq 0$ is a zero location in the $s$-plane. In this lesson, we will keep working this rule through additional numerator forms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Zeros of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding a Zero from a Linear Numerator",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the zero of $H(s)=\\frac{s-4}{s+3}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For this transfer function, the zero lies where the numerator is zero, so begin by writing $H(s)=0$ for $H(s)=\\frac{s-4}{s+3}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $H(s)=\\frac{N(s)}{D(s)}$ is a ratio, setting $H(s)=0$ requires $N(s)=0$ while keeping $D(s)\\neq 0$ at the same location. Here, the numerator is $s-4$, so reduce to $s-4=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s-4=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solve the linear equation to get $s=4$. Then check the denominator: $s+3\\neq0$ at $s=4$, so the candidate is valid and not canceled by a pole condition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The $s$-plane zero location is $s=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Zeros of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding Multiple Zeros from a Factored Numerator",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find all zeros of $H(s)=\\frac{(s+3)(s-4)}{s+5}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To find all zero locations in this step, use the definition that zeros make the transfer function equal to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set $H(s)=0$ for the given fraction, so the numerator must vanish while the denominator stays nonzero. This gives $(s+3)(s-4)/(s+5)=0$, and thus $(s+3)(s-4)=0$ with $s+5\\neq0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(s+3)(s-4)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the zero-product rule: a product is zero only when one factor is zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s+3=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s-4=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solve both equations to obtain $s=-3$ and $s=4$. Check $s=-5$ does not appear as either root, so both values are valid zero locations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Zeros of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding Zeros by Factoring a Quadratic Numerator",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find all zeros of $H(s)=\\frac{s^2-4s-5}{s^2+2s+1}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Find zeros where $H(s)=0$, so start from the cue equation $\\frac{s^2-4s-5}{s^2+2s+1}=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since this is a ratio, set the numerator to zero and keep the denominator nonzero at the zero locations. The first task is to factor the numerator.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s^2-4s-5=(s-5)(s+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the zero-product rule by setting each factor in the factored numerator equal to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s-5=0\\quad \\text{or}\\quad s+1=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solve both equations to get $s=5$ and $s=-1$. The zero set is $\\{5,-1\\}$, and both are valid because the denominator is finite at these values.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Zeros of a Transfer Function",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Zeros as Locations Where $H(s)$ Vanishes",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Identify the zeros of $H(s)=\\frac{(s+2)(s-4)}{(s+1)(s+3)}$ and interpret their meaning.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The zero condition is $H(s)=0$, so use this cue to focus on the numerator root condition first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For this transfer function, set the numerator to zero: $H(s)=\\frac{(s+2)(s-4)}{(s+1)(s+3)}\\Rightarrow (s+2)(s-4)=0$, because the denominator is checked separately.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(s+2)(s-4)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the factor rule: each factor can be zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s+2=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad s-4=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solving gives zeros at $s=-2$ and $s=4$. The denominator zeros $s=-1,-3$ are poles and are not zero locations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So in the $s$-plane, $s=-2$ and $s=4$ are where $H(s)=0$, while denominator roots indicate non-finite locations not in the zero set.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
